<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mieszko III the Old</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1126–1202</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mieszko III the Old was a Piast duke of Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, which he received under the testament of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> in 1138. He was also high duke of Poland, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> several times between 1173 and his death in 1202, but his harsh rule provoked revolts. The long struggle between his descendants and rival Piast lines shaped Greater Poland in the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mieszko-iii-the-old.jpg" alt="Mieszko III the Old" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mieszko_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mieszko was the fourth and second surviving son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> by his second wife Salomea, daughter of the German count Henry of Berg-Schelklingen. Under his father's testament of 1138, which set up a seniorate system, he received the newly established Duchy of Greater Poland. He had been duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>, where he had his main residence. His elder half-brother Władysław II was proclaimed high duke and overlord of the Seniorate Province at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>.</p><p>With his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iv-the-curly">Bolesław IV the Curly</a>, Mieszko opposed Władysław II in a series of conflicts from 1140. In 1146 the former high duke was excommunicated by Archbishop Jacob of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> and faced rebellion from his own subjects. By May 1146 all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was in the hands of the junior dukes, and Władysław fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Bolesław became high duke, while Mieszko kept Greater Poland and acted as his brother's ally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After Bolesław IV died on 3 April 1173, Mieszko became high duke by agnatic seniority. He remained in Greater Poland and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> to a governor, Henryk Kietlicz. His harsh taxes displeased the Lesser Polish magnates, and his efforts to strengthen ducal authority and limit the nobility and clergy brought wide opposition. In 1177 his eldest son Odon rebelled, fearing for his inheritance. He was supported by Bishop Gedko of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, Bolesław the Tall and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-the-just">Casimir II the Just</a>, and Casimir took Kraków as high duke. Mieszko fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a> and then left Poland.</p><p>Refused help by his son-in-law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and unable to pay the 10,000 pieces of silver Barbarossa asked, he was aided by another son-in-law, Duke Bogislaw I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. In 1181 he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a> and soon recovered all Greater Poland, reconciling with Odon in 1182. In 1191 he briefly retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, but Casimir regained it. After Casimir's death in 1194 Mieszko tried again, but at the Battle of Mozgawa on 13 September 1195 he was badly wounded and his son Bolesław of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1198 Mieszko negotiated his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> with Casimir's widow, Helen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a>, giving up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>. He was deposed again in 1199 and restored in a new settlement three years later, keeping the title of high duke with reduced powers. He died on 13 March 1202, having outlived all his siblings and all his sons but Władysław III Spindleshanks, who succeeded him in Greater Poland.</p><p>Medieval chroniclers often portrayed him as harsh and avaricious, while modern historians see a ruler who tried to impose stronger central government in a period that resisted it. His reign and repeated bids for supremacy illustrate the weaknesses of the Piast seniorate system and its part in prolonging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s political division.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mieszko III of Poland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the testament of Boleslaw III in 1138, his son <b>Mieszko III the Old</b> received Greater Poland, with Poznan and Gniezno, where the Piast state had begun.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>Krakow passed in turn to Boleslaw IV, <b>Mieszko III</b> and Casimir II the Just, and the seniority principle broke down as rival dukes fought for the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krakow">Duchy of Krakow</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.76,18.08&amp;year=1175" aria-label="Kalisz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4958 374 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Mieszko III the Old"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5408 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5408 644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.76,18.08&amp;year=1175">What was here in 1175? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
